\DOC LOCAL_MONTMUL_SM2_TAC

\TYPE {LOCAL_MONTMUL_SM2_TAC : int -> string list -> tactic}

\SYNOPSIS
Advances a symbolic-execution goal over one inlined Montgomery-multiplication
subroutine call, replacing the current state variable with a successor state
and adding an assumption characterising the product written to the destination
buffer.

\DESCRIBE
The first argument is an {int}: an offset/index selecting the code position of
the call being stepped over. The second is a {string list} naming the
destination buffer followed by the two input buffers of the multiplication;
these names determine the new assumption and the memory-read assumption that is
added. The tactic applies to a goal {A ?- eventually ... s} whose assumptions
describe the machine state {s}, and yields one subgoal whose conclusion
mentions a fresh successor state {s'}. All state-dependent assumptions about
the old state — program-counter value, loaded code, register contents,
memory-buffer contents, and the accumulated {MAYCHANGE} relation from the
initial state — are discharged and re-added for the new state, with the
{MAYCHANGE} relation extended by the destination buffer, a new memory-read
assumption giving the destination buffer's value in the new state, and a new
program-counter assumption; the previous program-counter assumption is dropped.
A purely arithmetic assumption is also added, stating conditionally (under a
bound on the product of the two inputs) that the destination value is the
Montgomery product of the inputs modulo the field prime. State-independent
assumptions already present, including those contributed by earlier steps, are
retained and reordered.
{
   A ?- t s
   =====================================================================  LOCAL_MONTMUL_SM2_TAC n [dest; in1; in2]
   A', new-state facts, product-characterisation ?- t s'
}

\COMMENTS
In all observed traces, only this single kind of transformation occurs, always
on goals of this shape and always producing one subgoal.

\ENDDOC
